HyperTrace experimental vendor import

Upstream: https://github.com/cadets/freebsd.git, branch hypertrace/main
Revision: 42466516653b1974ae8d8d3899e9d6972312322b
Committer date: 2024-08-23T18:45:24Z (author date differs).
FreeBSD base: db87c98168b1605f067d283fa36a710369c3849d
5BSD branch base: 1de2e37e9eff3a80d6605e546339f4ea4ebeb83f

upstream/ preserves the exact added/modified files relative to the fork's
last merged FreeBSD main revision, plus top-level and OpenSolaris licenses.
It is an overlay, NOT a standalone FreeBSD source checkout. All 1957 changed
paths are represented in upstream-manifest.json, including any deletions.
upstream.patch is the complete binary-capable delta against the pinned base.
Unmodified FreeBSD dependencies are obtained from the pinned upstream tree.
Copyright and license notices are retained; licenses vary by source file.

Do not build this old overlay as though it were a current 5BSD tree, or copy
its modified kernel files over modern 5BSD. Compiler, DTrace ABI, VMM and
network changes require review and integration against current interfaces.
The pristine import itself does not enable installation, module loading or
daemon startup. Native buildworld/kernel integration on the experimental
branch is tracked in STATUS.5BSD; it is not yet qualified end to end. The public branch appears dormant since 2024;
there may be other or private development not represented in this snapshot.

Keep upstream/ pristine. Local fixes belong in patches/ as an ordered series.
Apply that series to a separate working copy before compiling or testing.
This permits independent verification of upstream bytes and local changes.
The upstream README is historical; dtraced now resides in usr.sbin/dtraced
and uses C++, rather than the older C layout described in that README.

Integration requirements: port libdtrace ELF/type/relocation support and CLI;
port dtraced and its distribution protocol; integrate guest forwarding and
host guest-context handling; adapt virtio/VMM hypercalls to current bhyve;
qualify provider compatibility, isolation and fresh-session restart.
A vendor import or component test does not establish a working HyperTrace VM.

Scope revised 2026-10-19: tracing continuity across VM save/restore and
migration is no longer a release requirement. The supported target is a
fresh tracing session after startup or restore, with event gaps and reset
aggregations. The host must re-enable desired programs after old guest
probes and transport state have been cleared and a new identity established.
Ordinary attachment no longer requires the experimental rekey features.
The format-5 fresh-session restore coordinator is connected to staged device
reset, old-ring discard, and asynchronous authorization after guest ACTIVE.
It has component coverage but is not live-qualified. Keep the continuity code as experimental work,
not as a dependency of ordinary tracing. The current scope and retained
implementation history are in INTEGRATION-GATES.5BSD.md.

Fresh-session migration is connected to the production source command and
managed destination receiver. It remains experimental and is not live-qualified.
Both endpoints advertise FRESH only after authenticated certificate identities
and private, syncable recovery storage have been established. A tracing peer
cannot silently negotiate ordinary migration with a non-tracing peer.

The source IPC requires tls_certificate_fd, tls_private_key_fd, tls_trust_fd
and the 64-hex-digit tls_peer_sha256. The canonical private directory
/var/db/bhyve/migration must exist before bhyve starts. Bhyve opens and retains
it before Capsicum entry and before publishing its IPC thread. An optional
recovery_fddir must identify that same directory; it cannot replace the retained
root. Missing/invalid startup storage disables source migration, while ordinary
VM operation can continue. No absolute journal lookup occurs inside Capsicum.
Verification
failure never falls back to plaintext. Coordinated guest-probe and collector
stop precedes FSS1 capture and cutover. After accepted COMMIT, source INTENT,
exact-identity destruction, drained retirement proof, and durable RETIRED precede
RELEASE. Once retirement begins, errors never resume the source. Immutable
attempt ownership and a lifetime controller lock prevent competing recovery.
IPC command admission waits until main completes its startup VM operations.
Source quiescence waits for every vCPU thread to finish its userspace exit handler
and park before device pause and final memory copying. The acknowledgment wait
has a monotonic 30-second deadline. Failed CPU resume keeps the userspace fence
held; retirement wakes parked threads directly into shutdown.
Source quiescence drains console keyboard/pointer input before pausing devices;
only successful rollback restores input. Retirement makes that fence permanent.
Failed rollback leaves the source non-runnable. While retirement has never
begun, the controller automatically retries the idempotent device, vCPU and
console resume sequence (three attempts with 100 ms and 1 s backoff) and
reports repaired/repair_attempts. If that fails, a reversible quarantine admits
only status, recovery and the operator command migrate_repair (bhyvectl
--migrate-repair), which retries the same sequence and reopens ordinary IPC
only after success. Repeated migration requests cannot clear that state. Once
INTENT was attempted the quarantine is irreversible and repair is refused;
the retirement recovery command never infers retirement from a stopped state.
Expected destruction fences the ACPI power-button callback and stops vCPU threads
while preserving the migration controller.
The controller joins those threads before closing their VM context; a failed join
retains the context and durable INTENT for retry before RETIRED publication.
A configured GDB server participates in source migration through a debugger
fence. Migration is refused precisely, before quiesce, while a debugger owns
the guest (attached client, guest-memory breakpoints, pending single-step, or
a vCPU held by the server, including gdb.wait before first attach). Otherwise
new debugger connections are refused during cutover; only a rollback that
leaves the source runnable reopens them. Retirement fences the server
permanently and drops its vmctx alias before vCPU retirement and destruction.
On a destination, debugger connections are not serviced until the inbound
coordinator returns, because the mevent loop has not started.

The destination requires migrate.recovery_listen_fd (an already-listening
stream socket), migrate.recovery_fd (a private journal directory), and all four
migrate.tls settings: certificate_fd, private_key_fd, trust_fd, peer_sha256.
The managed receiver duplicates descriptors and creates its recovery worker
before accepting state. The VM startup fence must remain held. Destination firmware and control/sandbox
setup finish before receiving state. After RELEASE, the coordinator checks
kernel CPU resume before opening the userspace startup fence; a failure cannot
be acknowledged as successful execution. External console input is drained
before device restore and remains fenced until that resume succeeds. The unmanaged
entry points refuse tracing migration. The worker never creates or binds a
listener, retains committed state after ambiguous COMMIT/RELEASE failures, and
accepts authenticated reconnects even after resume to recover a lost ACK.
An uncertain resume, including failure of the initial RELEASE-driven attempt,
retains the image and remains quarantined; recovery never resumes it twice.

HMR2 records bind source/destination certificate fingerprints, VM lifetimes,
FSS1 metadata, the state digest, and a destination challenge. HMX1 reconciliation
is read-only; HXR1 execution applies ordered RELEASE/ACK persistence. Old trace
buffers and aggregates are discarded. Destination authorization is released
only through the fresh-state path after source retirement. Certificate or
attempt mismatch cannot authorize recovery.

A source controller can recover INTENT from exact kernel retirement evidence,
or use the optional authenticated identity-checked orphan retirement command
below. Missing names, PIDs, devices, and ioctls never prove retirement. A
collector crash without durable exit proof remains unresolved rather than
being counted as a stop; no procdesc, procctl reaper, kqueue or birth-identity
mechanism can prove a same-boot exit after the daemon lost its descriptor.
Only a verified registration from an earlier kernel boot (different
kern.boot_id) is accepted as proof that the collector, its descendants and its
kernel handle are gone, and it still does not publish a stop. A destination process crash loses its userspace/device
state and may lose RAM; HMR2 journals alone cannot reconstruct it. With the
opt-in migrate.recovery_image destination setting, a per-attempt controller lock
and a checksummed HDI1 image of the committed paused guest are durable before
STAGED, so COMMIT (and source retirement) can only follow a preserved image.
A successor started with migrate.reconstruct=CHALLENGE takes over only when the
lock shows no earlier controller is alive, the destination phase is exactly
STAGED (RELEASED precedes every resume), and the previous incarnation has an
exact kernel retirement witness. It records its own incarnation as HDS1 before
loading state, stays fenced, and resumes only through authenticated recovery
after durable source retirement. RELEASED/RUNNING attempts stay quarantined.
Without an image, destination recovery still requires its retained owner.
No live migration is qualified by the component tests or production links.

Completed collector stops retain their permanent FSI1 admission fence and FSD1
completion summary. Superseded FSA1 acknowledgments are retired after an exact
completed stop request, with a startup retry if cleanup was interrupted. Cleanup
never infers completion from a missing VM or process, never deletes incomplete
or differently bound acknowledgments, and retains program/cohort and shared
process/reaper/handle evidence. Storage errors defer cleanup without revoking
an already durable completion.

Fresh-session live acceptance checks (not yet executed)

Use a disposable VM and matched host/guest builds from the same source
revision. Record the revision, kernel configurations, negotiated features,
daemon/collector commands, VM identity and epoch, and complete logs. Verify
ordinary host and guest tracing before attempting any checkpoint. Do not
use the vendor overlay as either side of the test.

1. Start a bounded guest workload and a host collector. Verify expected
   events, correct guest attribution, and isolation from a second VM.
2. Request a stopped checkpoint while tracing is active. Verify exact guest
   stop acknowledgment and durable host collector completion before snapshot
   publication. Record the old identity; verify old work remains fenced.
3. Restore the format-5 checkpoint. Verify a distinct
   identity, empty old queues and partial programs, and no tracing execution
   before the fresh handshake and authenticated authorization complete.
4. Submit a newly authorized program. Verify new events arrive and old
   buffered events, program deliveries, and aggregate values do not reappear.
   Repeat stop and restart to exercise more than the first transition.
5. Interrupt the guest handshake, disconnect a collector during stop, and
   lose a release reply. Verify bounded failure, retained fences, and exact
   retry where implemented. A lost collector stop acknowledgment without a
   durable receipt is currently unresolved; refusal is not recovery success.
   The collector now commits proof on an independent authenticated connection
   after releasing the original reply's waiting worker, but before exit.
   Test loss of both the commit response and the
   original reply after durable publication; the old epoch must complete
   from its receipt without replay. Also crash before publication and verify
   the unresolved case retains its fence.
6. Restart the daemon at each durable stop boundary. Confirm old deliveries
   remain rejected and no missing acknowledgment is inferred from process
   disappearance. Preserve journals and logs for failure analysis.
7. Qualify migration separately on two matched hosts. Local epoch retirement
   does not prove remote retirement. That path and proof are implemented but require matched live evidence;
   same-host fresh restore alone does not qualify migration.

Repeat applicable cases for each supported architecture. Archive exit status,
event counts, identities, and failure logs alongside the native/build results.
Do not report these checks as passed merely because component fixtures pass.

Fresh migration initiation CLI (experimental)
--------------------------------------------
The snapshot-enabled bhyvectl frontend exposes the existing source migration
IPC through --migrate-fd. With an already-connected destination receive stream
on fd 3 and source certificate/key/trust PEM files on fds 4/5/6:

  bhyvectl --vm=NAME --migrate-fd=3 \
    --migrate-certificate-fd=4 --migrate-key-fd=5 --migrate-trust-fd=6 \
    --migrate-peer-sha256=DESTINATION_CERTIFICATE_SHA256

This template does not establish sockets, launch a destination, or grant live
execution authorization. Prepare a matched destination with receive_fd,
recovery_fd, recovery_listen_fd and mutual TLS configuration first, as described
above. Source canonical journal ownership and all existing device/HyperTrace
eligibility checks still apply. Only --vm and --rundir may accompany the
migration options. Initiation and recovery are mutually exclusive.

Success requires phase=COMPLETED, reason=NONE and source_runnable=false in the
controller reply. After a failed pre-COMMIT rollback that automatic repair
could not fix, "bhyvectl --vm=NAME --migrate-repair" retries it; success
requires repaired=true and source_runnable=true, and the controller refuses
once retirement began. A missing, malformed or lost reply is not success and does
not authorize source rollback. If retirement began, use --migrate-recover-fd
against the same retained owner; do not initiate a replacement attempt.
The CLI uses inherited descriptors without opening /dev/vmm or accepting a
replacement journal directory. Component checks do not qualify live migration.

The compiled frontend can be checked without a VM or tracing device:

  python3.12 contrib/hypertrace/tests/migration-cli-ipc.py \
    /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/initiation-cli-ipc \
    /path/to/built/snapshot-enabled/bhyvectl

This uses a disposable local IPC controller and test descriptors. Sixteen
initiation/recovery cases exercise actual libnv descriptor transfer and replies,
including lost responses, refusals and malformed completion fields. The result
records the executable hash. It does not exercise TLS or the production
migration controller and cannot qualify live behavior.

Fresh migration source recovery IPC (experimental)
-------------------------------------------------
The retained source process accepts cmd=migrate_recover with fd and the same
complete tls_certificate_fd, tls_private_key_fd, tls_trust_fd, tls_peer_sha256
credential fields used by migration. The connected peer must authenticate as
the original destination, using the original source certificate. The request
cannot select a VM incarnation, attempt binding or recovery directory. Those
come from retained process ownership; recovery_fddir is rejected. Responses
include confirmed, source_runnable, and error/error_code on failure. A lost
reply can be retried against that retained owner. Retirement quarantine remains
in force: only get_vm_pid and migrate_recover are accepted after retirement
begins. The recovery command never resumes the source.

This API has component tests and compiled production objects, not live VM
qualification. The separate recovery-only command below now handles an exited
controller when exact retirement can be established. Optional exact-identity orphan retirement is implemented as described below. Fresh initiation uses the mandatory production preconditions above.

The snapshot-enabled bhyvectl frontend now sends this request without opening
/dev/vmm, so a destroyed source VM is not an obstacle to contacting its retained
process. Supply inherited descriptors, for example (3 is an already-connected
stream socket, 4/5/6 are certificate/key/trust PEM files):

  bhyvectl --vm=NAME --migrate-recover-fd=3 \
    --migrate-certificate-fd=4 --migrate-key-fd=5 --migrate-trust-fd=6 \
    --migrate-peer-sha256=DESTINATION_CERTIFICATE_SHA256

Only --vm and --rundir may accompany these recovery options. The command does
not establish a network connection or choose a replacement recovery directory.
A successful exit requires confirmed=true and source_runnable=false in the
source process reply. Failure or a lost reply does not permit source rollback;
retry against the same retained owner. This does not recover a source process
that has exited and does not enable fresh migration initiation.

Source recovery after process exit (experimental)
------------------------------------------------
The snapshot-enabled bhyve binary has a one-shot recovery path that exits before
normal VM initialization. An authorized operator supplies the original source
VM ID and epoch from trusted local migration records, never from the remote
peer. With connected socket fd 3 and certificate/key/trust fds 4/5/6:

  bhyve -o migrate.recover_source_fd=3 \
    -o migrate.recover_source_vmid=SOURCE_VMID \
    -o migrate.recover_source_epoch=SOURCE_EPOCH \
    -o migrate.tls.certificate_fd=4 \
    -o migrate.tls.private_key_fd=5 \
    -o migrate.tls.trust_fd=6 \
    -o migrate.tls.peer_sha256=DESTINATION_CERTIFICATE_SHA256

This is a reviewable invocation template, not a command run by these tests.
Do not append a VM name or restore/receiver settings. Recovery uses only the
private /var/db/bhyve/migration root, validates immutable source ownership,
serializes with other controllers and authenticates the original certificate
pair. INTENT needs an exact kernel retirement witness; durable retirement does
not require reopening the query device. Errors never authorize source rollback.
Without a name hint, a still-live orphan remains unresolved. To permit its
retirement, supply -o migrate.recover_source_name=ORIGINAL_NAME. The name is
only a lookup hint: the kernel atomically verifies the original VMID and epoch
before scheduling destruction. Authentication of the original certificate pair
and durable INTENT precede that request; an exact drained retirement witness
and durable RETIRED still precede RELEASE. Missing names, devices, or unsupported
ioctls do not prove retirement. This command never loads modules, starts or
resumes a VM, or deletes journals. Source recovery still requires a destination
recovery owner: the retained original, or a successor reconstructed from a
preserved image as described above (bhyve(8), "Destination reconstruction").

Matched live-test evidence matrix (prepared, not executed)
---------------------------------------------------------
tests/fresh-session-live-matrix.json defines 25 cases and the evidence
required for each. Every case starts as not-run. Its execution_authorized=false
records that this preparation grants no permission to operate hosts, VMs,
daemons, modules or tracing devices. Obtain authorization for the named test
environment and fault actions before execution. Record results separately from
this template, with source revision and dirty-tree digest, kernel configurations,
loaded module identities, binary/library hashes, guest image identity, negotiated
features, exact commands, exit status, and monotonic event ordering per host.
Do not compare unsynchronized timestamps between hosts as ordering proof; bind
COMMIT/RELEASE and journal records by challenge, identity and certificate pair.
Preserve journal copies and fault timelines without exporting private keys.

Use distinct old/new workload tags and distinct aggregate initial values. Merely
seeing post-restore events does not prove the absence of stale buffered data.
Capture queue/partial-program discard and authorization boundaries as well as
collector output. Test a second VM concurrently to check attribution isolation.
For source-retirement faults, record independent source execution observations
and destination resume counts; a successful client exit alone is insufficient.

The matrix distinguishes transport loss before delivery from a lost reply after
peer action. Live injection controls for these boundaries must be prepared and
validated in the authorized lab; the local TLS fixtures do not supply live VM
fault injection. Migration cases require the documented production prerequisites
and explicit lab authorization; conditional admission is implemented but is not
live-qualified.
A refused still-live orphan is a verified safety refusal, not a recovery pass.
ARM64 and RISC-V snapshot/migration cases remain unsupported; apply their baseline
tracing cases without treating AMD64 build results as cross-architecture proof.
The QEMU smoke helper does not emulate the bhyve VirtIO DTrace device and cannot
qualify this host/guest matrix. No live result is currently claimed here.

Probe-context guest memory
--------------------------
On amd64, the DTrace hypercall tries the guest pmap mutex before entering
HyperTrace and releases it after leaving probe context. Contention drops the
event. Lockstat/WITNESS instrumentation therefore runs outside guest DIF.
The guest-memory callbacks copy only resident managed RAM under that lock;
they never call vm_gpa_hold or the VM fault machinery. Guest user access
checks U/S and R/W through the guest tables; writes additionally require host
NPT/EPT write permission and mark the managed pages dirty. Both pages of a
cross-page copy are validated before either is modified. Missing mappings
fail the access instead of faulting pages in. This can lose events under
memory pressure or contention and is not a guarantee of lossless tracing.

The dtrace_vm_subr and vmm_hypertrace suites in tests/coverage-kernel.py cover
resident copies, permissions, no partial copy on failure, lock ownership,
and contended hypercall admission. They execute production translation units
against synthetic kernel state, without loading modules or running VMs.
